prunedssa_x86_64 -- the GH-1034 heritage-marker fixture for options `readonlycalls`
and `prunedssa` (tests/stages/kuna-prunedssa.xml).

What it is
  A 32,584-byte x86-64 ELF built from prunedssa_x86_64.c, which is generated
  straight-line C. `churn` makes 256 calls to `sink`, reads one word of the
  read-only table `tab` (in .rodata, defined in inline assembly so the compiler
  cannot fold the reads) before each call, selects with conditional moves, and
  copies a 128-byte struct with an inline `rep movsb` every fourth step.
  `escaped` hands the address of each of 64 stack structs to `reg`, a 64-slot
  version of tests/hang-repro/escaped_locals whose address-tied slots neither
  option changes.

Why it is vendored
  The op counts the stage test pins depend on the exact code layout, so the
  binary is checked in rather than built at test time (CI's gcc 15 would lay the
  functions out differently).

Where it came from
  gcc 11.4.0 (Ubuntu 11.4.0-1ubuntu1~22.04.3), GNU ld 2.38, x86-64 Linux:

      gcc -O2 -fno-pie -no-pie -fno-inline -fstack-reuse=none -o prunedssa_x86_64 prunedssa_x86_64.c

  sha256 a19b79b80b066795a887f194d4b365d0b484e5cfedb4502c3cc96975d32d9ac0.
  Run with no arguments it prints 6093589510555084827 11072.
